# Lesson 4, new exercise: a finesse you don't need. Dummy's clubs take care of the diamond queen, so there's no need to risk it.
title: No need to finesse
id: L04-11
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.7
student: S
board: 75
dealer: S
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  762    Q975   74     AKQJ
  E  QT94   T2     8652   T87
  S  853    AKJ84  AQ3    92
  W  AKJ    63     KJT9   6543

auction:
  S 1H     = 13+ points, at least four hearts
     not *: 14 points and five hearts: open your long suit at the one level.
  W Pass
  N 3H     = 4+ hearts, an opening hand or better: game forcing
  E Pass
  S 4H     = Game
     not *: Partner's 3♥ is forcing to game. Bid it.
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♥ and partner jumped to 3♥, showing four hearts and an opening
  hand or better, so you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid hearts
  first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 4
  spades: 3 = Three small spades opposite three small spades.
  hearts: 0 = Solid.
  diamonds: 1 = The queen, unless East has the king. Dummy can ruff your third diamond.
  clubs: 0 = Your two small clubs are covered by dummy's ace and king.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♥. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: The diamond finesse is one way to get rid of a loser. Is there a surer way?
play:
  1   W SA  N S2  E S4  S S3
      note: Four losers, one too many. The diamond finesse is one way to save a trick, but there's a sure way: dummy has four club winners and you have only two clubs. The third and fourth clubs can take your two diamonds.
  2   W SK  N S6  E S9  S S5
  3   W SJ  N S7  E SQ  S S8
      note: Three spade losers, as you counted. You can't afford another.
  4   E H2  S H4  W H3  N H5
      note: East switches to a trump. Draw trumps before you cash the clubs, so nobody can ruff them.
  5   N H7  E HT  S HA  W H6
      note: Both opponents followed twice, so trumps are drawn.
  6   S C2  W C3  N CA  E C7
      note: Now the clubs. Lead to dummy's ace.
  7   N CK  E C8  S C9  W C4
      note: And the king.
  8   N CQ  E CT  S D3  W C5
      note: You're out of clubs, so throw a diamond on dummy's queen.
  9   N CJ  E ST  S DQ*  W C6
      note: And the ♦Q on the jack. No diamond finesse needed.
      DQ key: Throw the queen. Now you have no diamond loser at all, and you never needed the finesse.
      DQ not *: Did you count your losers? Throw the ♦Q: your ace will take care of the last diamond, and no finesse is needed.
  10  N D4  E D2  S DA  W D9
      note: Over to your ♦A.
  11  S HK  W DT  N H9  E D5
      note: The rest are yours.
  12  S HJ  W DJ  N HQ  E D6
  13  N D7  E D8  S H8  W DK
      note: 10 tricks: 4♥ made.

play recap:
  The plan: you counted four losers, one too many. Rather than hope East held
  the ♦K for a finesse, you threw your diamonds away on dummy's extra club
  winners, a sure thing.

  West had the ♦K, so the finesse would have lost, and with three spades
  already gone, you'd have been down one. When you have a sure way to get rid
  of a loser, take it, and save the finesse for when you need it.
